{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792775613,"nonce":"OV75JvIXLtnMqcPJ6wzXCw+LIgc=","message":"ChimpMonk CrowdGc (chimpmonk example/src/CrowdGc.hx, headless, native hybrid, ash 0.1.12 Oct 23 16:57 build, machine loaded at 14 to 19): 1x/4x/16x = 256/1024/4096 boxes and 8/32/128 skinned figures, 30 s fly-through. Allocation inside the window 0.056/0.074/0.033 MB (0.002 MB/s): the simulation hot path allocates almost nothing, so the GC never runs during frames. Collections 4/4/5 (1 minor each), two of them the harness's own explicit majors, which took 10/130/135 ms; the worst automatic pause 2.4/1.7/16.2 ms. Live at end 16/59/146 MB. Frame work p50/p95/p99 32.6/58.4/114 ms, 65.6/117/167 ms, 375/815/1002 ms; every scale is over 16.6 ms, from compute in world.step, not pauses. 64x never left the warmup in 14 minutes. One GC finding: at 64x a minor collection during setup took 325.7 ms (mark 308.9 ms, old root ranges 10,026 = 135.4 MB, live 455.6 MB): a minor treats the whole young-filled heap as dirty roots when a program builds a large structure at once. Conclusion: for this workload the GC is not the limit; ChimpMonk's physics compute is (and the loaded machine inflates it). A GC testbed with real allocation would be the editor/ashui orbit stress (18 MB/s). Open: whether world.step is slow because of ash codegen (needs an unloaded NUC/Mac run), and the large-minor-pause-at-setup case.","files":null}]}